WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
Building a database involves

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.425
working with substantial
amounts of data.

3
00:00:04.425 --> 00:00:06.540
But how do you organize
your data so that you can

4
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.045
find exactly what you need
and make sense of it.

5
00:00:09.045 --> 00:00:11.640
With SQL, you can
create a table within

6
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.475
your database to hold
and organize your data.

7
00:00:14.475 --> 00:00:17.460
In this video, you'll learn
how to create tables in

8
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.880
a database management
system using SQL syntax.

9
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.710
Let's begin with
a look at the SQL

10
00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.855
create table statement syntax.

11
00:00:24.855 --> 00:00:27.690
I begin the statement
with the keywords Create

12
00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:31.305
Table to let SQL know that I
want to create a new table.

13
00:00:31.305 --> 00:00:34.200
I then add the name of the
table I want to create.

14
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.870
Finally, I add a
pair of parentheses.

15
00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.595
Within these parentheses,

16
00:00:38.595 --> 00:00:40.380
I type the name of
each column that

17
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.250
must be included
within the table,

18
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.690
followed by its
respective datatype.

19
00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.970
Now that you're familiar
with the syntax,

20
00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:48.905
let's look at it in action.

21
00:00:48.905 --> 00:00:51.815
Be aware that before
you can create tables,

22
00:00:51.815 --> 00:00:54.245
you must already have a
database on the server.

23
00:00:54.245 --> 00:00:55.880
In other words, you can't build

24
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.510
tables if there's no
database to create them in.

25
00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.200
Let's assume that I already have

26
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.865
a database ready to work with.

27
00:01:01.865 --> 00:01:04.520
In this example, I'll
create a customer table in

28
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.260
the bookstore database to store

29
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.295
customer data like names
and phone numbers.

30
00:01:09.295 --> 00:01:11.750
First, I write a SQL statement

31
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.895
that contains the
create table command,

32
00:01:13.895 --> 00:01:15.545
followed by the
name of the table.

33
00:01:15.545 --> 00:01:17.210
In this case customers.

34
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.675
Then I add an open parenthesis.

35
00:01:19.675 --> 00:01:21.650
Is within this
parenthesis that I

36
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.975
need to create my own columns.

37
00:01:23.975 --> 00:01:26.210
I write the name of
my first column,

38
00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:27.725
which is customer name.

39
00:01:27.725 --> 00:01:30.875
This is followed by
the datatype VARCHAR.

40
00:01:30.875 --> 00:01:32.750
This datatype means that

41
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.495
the column can hold
data of any type.

42
00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:39.470
I then add a numeric value
within a pair of parenthesis.

43
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.600
Then I add a comma
and write the name of

44
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.730
the second column,
which is phone number.

45
00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:45.695
I add into the datatype

46
00:01:45.695 --> 00:01:48.005
so that only whole
numbers can be stored.

47
00:01:48.005 --> 00:01:51.370
Then I add a closing
parenthesis and a semicolon.

48
00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:53.970
Finally, I execute
the statement.

49
00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.990
The customers table is now
stored in the database.

50
00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.540
In this video, you learned
how to create tables in

51
00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.600
a database using SQL
syntax. Well done.